I've won at Rogue three times recently.  Here's what I think.

First, you don't "ascend" at Rogue, that's a Nethack term, where you
"ascend to demigod(dess)" upon victory.  Victorious Rogues are called
Winners, or Total Winners.  (In this version of Rogue, all winners are
total.)

Rogue is hard to win because, unlike Nethack, player ability +
equipment power tends to improve slower than monster power does. 
Also, you're much more at the mercy of the random number generator in
Rogue, in that there is no way to get the items you really want other
than to luck upon them.  There's also a lot fewer ways to identify
things in Rogue.

While it's possible to win almost every Nethack game (over at alt.org
there's a player who's ascended something like 13 characters in 14
games), in Rogue there are sometimes games in which you really are
screwed, and no way to know for certain which those are.

The version of Rogue I've won at, it should be said, is somewhat
easier than the one you're playing.  I won at Rogue Clone IV, which
doesn't have dark rooms and does have scrolls of Protect Armor and
long-lasting Potions of Detect Monster.  (On the other hand, it also
has Potions of Hallucination, fewer weapon types, much more dangerous
Ice Monsters, fast Kestrels and Griffins, and occaisional Big Rooms on
lower levels.)

But anyway, the keys to winning at Rogue, in my opinion, are these:

1. Conserve your scrolls of Identify.  If you know what Remove Curse
is, then you have a little leeway in IDing rings through
experimentation.  If your armor is protected or leather, then consider
testing wands on Aquators/Rust Monsters (though be ready to zap it
again if the monster turns into something mean).  And whenever you
find an unID'd scroll on the ground, don't pick it up until you've
tried to lead a monster over it; if a monster refused to step on a
scroll it's Scare Monster, perhaps the most valuable type of item in
the game other than a wand of Slow Monster.

2. Take advantage of extra food.  In the version of Rogue I'm playing,
food consumption decreases dramatically once you pick up the Amulet of
Yendor and start going upstairs.  (This may not be true of Epyx
Rogue!)  So, if you have a surplus of food on the way down, you may be
able to spend some time building levels, or wearing rings, in order to
increase your chances.

3. Take *extra* advantage of Scrolls of Scare Monster.  The proper way
to use these is to drop it when in trouble.  So long as you're
standing on a Scroll of Scare Monsters, no monster will attack you,
though they will helpfully stand on adjacent spaces while you beat
them up.  They're great for clearing out Party Rooms (aka Zoos).  The
only monster that can truly harm you when you're standing on one of
these is a Dragon (who may flame you).  If a Medusa confuses you while
you're standing on one, just use the period key to rest in place,
which should prevent you from accidently moving off the space.  By the
way, Nethack's Scare Monster scrolls have the same effect, making them
a nice makeshift Elbereth in tight spots.

Since Scare Monster scrolls protect you so utterly, you should always
take great advantage of them when forced to drop one.  If a Griffin
has you down to three hit points, don't just drop the scroll to
escape, *kill* it!  And kill any other monsters you find on the level
while you're at it!

In Rogue Clone IV, you can move onto an item without picking it up
with the Move command (Shift-M followed by direction), which will
allow you to use one scroll multiple times so long as you don't try to
pick it up.  Trying to pick up a previously-dropped scroll will cause
it to disintegrate.  I don't know if the Move trick works in Epyx
Rogue, but in both versions, you can use a Scroll of Scare Monster you
haven't picked up yet by moving onto its space with a full inventory.

4. Take advantage of Wands of Slow Monster.  Play your cards right,
and each charge in such wands is one dead monster.  The idea is that
you zap a particularly challenging monster, then alternate between
hitting it and running away.  Slowed monsters can only act every other
turn, so you use the turns they waste to strike, and the turns they
act to run.

5. There's a trick to handling Party Rooms, those annoying rooms full
of items and monsters that all wake up when you enter.  Monsters don't
have much better eyesight in corridors than you do.  So what you do
is, run away down the hall and try to find a branching corridor, or
barring that a small room with more than two exits.  Those monsters
who are more than two spaces away from you won't know which way you
went when they reach an intersection (they don't use the motion of
their fellow monsters as a clue to which way you went).  You can use
this to kill the monsters a few at a time, and also to circle back
around to the now-empty room and its abandoned riches.

6. You really need to come up with a good item IDing strategy.  All
games of Rogue involve a good amount of IDing by experimentation, and
that necessarily entails some risks.

For example, don't start quaffing potions until you've built up four
or five different types.  Why?  Because potions of Healing cure
blindness, and Healing, Extra Healing and Poison all cure
hallucination!  Blindness and Hallucination are the two most hazardous
potions, so if you have a potion of Extra Healing on hand you're in
much better shape to be randomly quaffing things.

The bad scrolls tend to be less dangerous, but several of the good
ones influence some piece of equipment you're wearing.  Thus, testing
out scrolls should probably wait until you've found better armor and a
better weapon, so you can wear/wield it while you're experimenting
with scrolls in case they turn out to be Enchant/Vorpalize
Weapon/Remove Curse.  Note that enchantment scrolls always remove any
curse on the object they operate upon, and that Remove Curse scrolls
affect everything in your pack.

Scrolls of Identify, in general, are too valuable to waste on weapons
and armor unless you've got a surplus of them.  (Armor pluses get
revealed upon wearing them anyway.)  They're best saved for rings
(several of them are very hard to figure out on their own) or wands
(to avoid wasting charges on a possible Wand of Slow Monster).


Rogue can be beaten, but it'll probably take dozens of tries on Rogue
Clone, and maybe hundreds in Epyx Rogue (those dark rooms are a real
problem).  Just remember, when you get sick of getting killed over and
over by Medusas and Griffins within sight of the Amulet, Nethack will
always be waiting there with open arms when you're ready to play a
gentler, more reasonable game....

